// pf_shape.vfl - PRIM wrangle, between `stamp()` and the setback inset.
//
// §12.6 B1's SHAPE ops - `shapeL` and `shapeU` - the half of the lot ->
// footprint vocabulary that changes the footprint's TOPOLOGY rather than its
// dimensions.  `setback`, `identity` and `offset` are all the same per-edge
// inset with different numbers and are `pf_inset.vfl`'s; `shapeO` is a ring,
// which `pf_mass.vfl` already builds from `volumeTopology.courtyardDepthM`
// (§12.6 B1: "the courtyard that `shapeO` would cut is produced in B2 by
// insetting the footprint a second time with the same rule"), so `stamp()`
// routes it there and this file is not asked.  §7's finding is that lot ->
// footprint is ONE pluggable stage; this is the pluggable part of it.
//
// ⭐ THE SCOPE IS THE LOT'S OWN ORIENTED PLAN BOX.  CityEngine's
// `shapeL`/`shapeU` are defined on the shape's SCOPE, and a scope is an
// ORIENTED box - so an AXIS-ALIGNED box is not "CityEngine's behaviour too",
// it is a different operation that happens to agree on an axis-aligned lot.
// ⛔ THE FIRST BUILD MADE THAT CLAIM AND IT WAS FALSE, MEASURED: on a 30 x 24
// lot rotated about its own centre with `shapeL(14, 12, at = 2)`, the
// axis-aligned box-L put three footprint corners outside the lot at 15°
// (worst 4.084 m), four at 30° (9.392 m) and four at 45° (11.465 m), with all
// four `pf_warn_*` at 0.  On a rotated lot CityEngine's notch stays inside the
// parcel and that one did not.  The box below is the minimum-area plan box
// over the lot's OWN EDGE DIRECTIONS, and on an axis-aligned lot it is
// bit-identical to the box this file used to build.
// ⛔ AND "WHICH FOR A RECTANGLE AT ANY ANGLE IS THE LOT ITSELF" IS ONLY HALF
// TRUE, WHICH IS HOW THE SECOND DEFECT SURVIVED A ROUND.  It is true of the
// BOX - the four corners as a SET - and false of the CORNER INDEXING, which is
// what `at`, the four axes and every `pf_face_role` are read off.  A rectangle
// has four min-area candidate directions and they name the SAME box with its
// corners numbered four different ways, so an op that agrees on the box can
// still cut the notch at the wrong corner.  What decides the indexing is the
// tie-break, and that is the block below.
//
// ⛔ AND THE FOOTPRINT MAY NOT LEAVE THE LOT, WHICH IS A GUARD AND NOT A
// COMMENT.  A lot that is not a rectangle has a scope box LARGER than itself,
// so a notch cut out of that box can still escape.  The escape is measured
// HERE, against the lot, because this is the last node that still HAS the lot:
// `pf_collapse.vfl` measures containment against `_p0`, which `pf_inset.vfl`
// writes AFTER this file has already replaced the outline, so downstream the
// only thing left to compare against is the box-L itself.  ⚠️ Measuring it
// downstream would also be too late to repair: `pf_mass.vfl`'s degraded
// fallback rebuilds on `_p0`, i.e. on the escaped footprint.  A notch that
// escapes therefore sets `_shapebad` and LEAVES THE FOOTPRINT ALONE, exactly
// as a notch that does not fit does - §2.2, advisory and never a refusal, and
// the building comes out on its lot carrying
// `pf_warn_footprint_collapsed`.
//
// THE GUARD TESTS CORNERS AND EDGES, and for one build it tested only corners
// while SAYING SO - a stated limitation with its magnitude left unmeasured.
// It is 5.800 m on an ordinary slotted parcel and the standing check shared the
// hole; the numbers and the crossing test are at the guard itself, below.
//
// ⚠️ NON-CONVEX OUTPUT IS THE POINT, not an accident: §12.6 B1 says it is
// "legal and expected", and G2's whole gate is a reflex corner.  Both ops
// manufacture exactly the reflex corners §5 Theme 4 says every surveyed
// generator fails at - one for `shapeL`, two for `shapeU`.
//
// FACE ROLES ARE INHERITED BY OUTWARD NORMAL, and it is one rule for every
// manufactured edge including the reflex ones: an output edge takes the role -
// and therefore the setback - of the INPUT edge whose outward normal is
// closest to its own.  The four axes it matches against are the SCOPE's, not
// the world's, so the rule rotates with the lot.  On a rectangle at any angle
// that is exact and unique (four edges, four normals).  On anything else it is
// a nearest-normal match and can hand two output edges the same input edge's
// role; stated, not fixed, because the input class that would fix it is a
// per-edge role on a shape B0 has not been asked to produce.
//
// ⚠️ ONE PRIM PER POINT, the same assumption `pf_inset.vfl` states: the ring's
// points are moved in place, so a point shared with another lot would move for
// both.  Lots are separate loops, not a shared planar graph.
//
// WHAT THIS CANNOT SEE: whether the notch it was asked for is the notch the
// artist meant.  It can only see that the notch FITS and that it stays inside
// the lot, which are the two guards below; either one sets `_shapebad` -
// which `pf_collapse.vfl` folds into `pf_warn_footprint_collapsed`, §2.2's
// advisory and never a refusal - and leaves the footprint alone.

i@_shapebad = 0;
int op = i@_shape;
if (op == 0) return;

int pts[] = primpoints(0, @primnum);
int n = len(pts);
if (n < 3) { i@_shapebad = 1; return; }

float w = f@_shape_w, dep = f@_shape_d;
int at = ((i@_shape_at % 4) + 4) % 4;

// Winding, the lot outline and the incoming per-edge data - all read BEFORE
// anything moves.
float a = 0.0, y = 0.0;
vector lot[];
string inrole[];
float ininset[], inback[];
resize(lot, n); resize(inrole, n); resize(ininset, n); resize(inback, n);
for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {
    vector p = point(0, "P", pts[k]);
    vector r = point(0, "P", pts[(k + 1) % n]);
    a += p.x * r.z - r.x * p.z;
    lot[k] = p;
    y = p.y;
    // Typed locals: `vertex()` is untyped and the assignments below are
    // otherwise ambiguous (dev-loop trap list).
    int vi = vertexindex(0, @primnum, k);
    string rl = vertex(0, "pf_face_role", vi);
    float in = vertex(0, "_inset", vi);
    float sb = vertex(0, "pf_setback", vi);
    inrole[k] = rl; ininset[k] = in; inback[k] = sb;
}
float sgn = (a > 0.0) ? 1.0 : -1.0;
// THE LOT'S OWN ORIGIN.  Everything that decides the frame below is measured
// from it, and `SCOPE_REL` is the band two candidate boxes have to differ by
// before one is called smaller - both are answered in the block that follows.
vector org = set(lot[0].x, y, lot[0].z);
float SCOPE_REL = 1e-3;

// THE SCOPE.  A minimum-area enclosing rectangle of a convex polygon always
// has a side collinear with one of its edges, so trying every edge direction
// finds it; on a non-convex lot it finds the smallest box over the same
// candidate set, which is a bound and is stated as one.
//
// ⛔ EVERY EDGE OF A RECTANGLE GIVES THE SAME BOX, SO THE COMPARISON AND THE
// TIE-BREAK - NOT THE AREA - DECIDE WHICH CORNER `at` NAMES.  For one build
// the band was an ABSOLUTE `1e-6` while the float32 spread over geometrically
// identical candidate directions is 3.05e-04 m² at the origin and 9.77e-04 m²
// at x = 200 on a 720 m² lot, so the band was NEVER REACHED and rounding error
// picked the frame: 68 of 181 half-degree orientations cut the notch at the
// WRONG CORNER of the lot - a legal six-corner L, entirely inside the parcel,
// +2.0 m clearance, all four `pf_warn_*` at 0, and every `pf_face_role` and
// therefore every setback on a different edge.  ⛔ AND WIDENING THE BAND ALONE
// MAKES IT WORSE, MEASURED (68 -> 90 of 181): the old tie-break folded each
// direction into the +x half-plane and took the one nearest +x, which is a
// question about the WORLD and flips at 45°, so under a band that is actually
// reached every lot past 45° comes out wrong by construction.  Three changes,
// each measured rather than argued:
//   (1) THE CANDIDATE AREAS ARE MEASURED FROM `org`, the lot's own first
//       point, which takes the world offset out of the dot products.  Measured
//       relative spread over the 181-angle sweep, WITHOUT it -> WITH it:
//       x = 0      4.24e-07 -> 3.39e-07
//       x = 200    1.36e-06 -> 5.09e-07
//       x = 5 000  3.46e-05 -> 7.63e-06
//       x = 20 000 1.47e-04 -> 2.89e-05
//       ⚠️ IT DOES NOT MAKE THE NOISE FLAT AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS COMMENT
//       CLAIMED IT DID.  `P` is float32, so a lot 20 km out is ALREADY
//       quantised to ~2 mm before this file sees it and its candidate boxes
//       genuinely differ; the subtraction removes the cancellation, worth ~5x
//       there, and nothing removes the input's own resolution.
//   (2) THE BAND IS RELATIVE, `SCOPE_REL` of the area, so it does not decay
//       with distance the way an absolute one does: measured margin over the
//       noise above is 2 950x at x = 0, 1 965x at x = 200, 131x at x = 5 000
//       and 35x at x = 20 000.  Every edge of a rectangle therefore ties and
//       the tie-break below is consulted.  ⚠️ THE CEILING IS REAL AND IS
//       float32 `P`'s, not this band's: the margin falls ~linearly with the
//       distance from the world origin, so a lot some hundreds of km out would
//       need a double-precision `P` and not a wider band.
//   (3) THE TIE IS BROKEN ON THE LOT AND NEVER ON THE WORLD: the LONGEST edge
//       wins, and edges of equal length go to the first in the lot's own RING
//       ORDER.  Both rotate with the lot, so the frame rotates with the lot -
//       which is the whole property "nearest +x" could not have.  There is no
//       fold into a half-plane any more, deliberately: `lot[e] -> lot[e+1]` is
//       a direction the lot itself supplies, and folding it discarded exactly
//       the information that makes the frame equivariant past 90°.
// ⚠️ WHAT THE RING ORDER STILL DECIDES, AND IT IS THE SHAPE'S OWN SYMMETRY
// RATHER THAN A LEAK.  A rectangle is unchanged by a 180° rotation about its
// centre and a square by a 90° one, so NO rule reading only the lot's geometry
// can name one corner of either: the two long edges are antiparallel and
// identical, and there is nothing to prefer one to the other.  The longest-edge
// rule resolves everything geometry CAN resolve - the u axis, and therefore
// what `widthM` and `depthM` mean - and the ring's starting vertex resolves
// exactly the residue.  Measured on a 30 x 24 lot: rotating its VERTEX LIST by
// 1 or 2 places gives the 180°-rotated frame at all 181 angles, by 3 places the
// same frame; on a trapezoid, whose longest edge is unique, every rotation of
// the vertex list gives the same frame.  It is deterministic and it rotates
// with the lot, and it is the price of equivariance: the rule that pinned the
// sign against the WORLD instead is the one that put 68 of 181 orientations at
// the wrong corner - and 493 of 721 over the full circle, where folding into
// the +x half-plane is discontinuous at 90°.
vector bu = set(1.0, 0.0, 0.0);
float bestar = 1e30, bestlen = -1.0;
for (int e = 0; e < n; e++) {
    vector d = lot[(e + 1) % n] - lot[e];
    d.y = 0.0;
    float ln = length(d);
    if (ln < 1e-9) continue;
    d /= ln;
    vector wv = set(-d.z, 0.0, d.x);
    float d0 = 1e18, d1 = -1e18, w0 = 1e18, w1 = -1e18;
    for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {
        vector q = lot[k] - org;
        float dd = dot(q, d), ww = dot(q, wv);
        d0 = min(d0, dd); d1 = max(d1, dd);
        w0 = min(w0, ww); w1 = max(w1, ww);
    }
    float ar = (d1 - d0) * (w1 - w0);
    float band = bestar * SCOPE_REL;
    int better = (ar < bestar - band) ? 1 : 0;
    if (!better && ar < bestar + band)
        better = (ln > bestlen * (1.0 + SCOPE_REL)) ? 1 : 0;
    if (better) { bu = d; bestlen = ln; bestar = min(bestar, ar); }
}
vector uax = bu, vax = set(-bu.z, 0.0, bu.x);
float umin = 1e18, umax = -1e18, vmin = 1e18, vmax = -1e18;
for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {
    vector q = lot[k] - org;
    float du = dot(q, uax), dv = dot(q, vax);
    umin = min(umin, du); umax = max(umax, du);
    vmin = min(vmin, dv); vmax = max(vmax, dv);
}
float ex = umax - umin, ez = vmax - vmin;

// Axis 0 = -v, 1 = +u, 2 = +v, 3 = -u - the scope's four outward directions,
// which on an axis-aligned lot are -z, +x, +z, -x.  `src[ax]` is the input
// edge whose outward normal best matches that axis, and it is what carries
// roles across.
vector axis[] = array(-vax, uax, vax, -uax);
int src[];
float bestdot[];
resize(src, 4); resize(bestdot, 4);
for (int q = 0; q < 4; q++) { src[q] = 0; bestdot[q] = -2.0; }
for (int e = 0; e < n; e++) {
    vector d = set(lot[(e + 1) % n].x - lot[e].x, 0.0,
                   lot[(e + 1) % n].z - lot[e].z);
    if (length(d) < 1e-9) continue;
    vector no = normalize(set(d.z, 0.0, -d.x)) * sgn;
    for (int q = 0; q < 4; q++) {
        float dt = dot(no, axis[q]);
        if (dt > bestdot[q]) { bestdot[q] = dt; src[q] = e; }
    }
}

// The box, positively wound in (x, z): corner i starts edge i, and edge i's
// outward normal is axis i.  Edges 0 and 2 run `ex`, edges 1 and 3 run `ez`,
// which is what the parity below reads - the first build tested `eout.x` and
// so asked a world question about a scope quantity.
vector box[] = array(uax * umin + vax * vmin + org,
                     uax * umax + vax * vmin + org,
                     uax * umax + vax * vmax + org,
                     uax * umin + vax * vmax + org);
float extout = (at % 2 == 0) ? ex : ez;
float extin = (at % 2 == 0) ? ez : ex;
vector ring[];

if (op == 1) {
    // shapeL: remove a `widthM` x `depthM` rectangle at box corner `at`.
    // WIDTH is measured along the edge LEAVING the corner, DEPTH back along
    // the edge ARRIVING at it, so on the near corner of an axis-aligned lot
    // they read as the notch's x and z extents.
    vector ein = normalize(box[at] - box[(at + 3) % 4]);
    vector eout = normalize(box[(at + 1) % 4] - box[at]);
    if (!(w > 1e-3 && dep > 1e-3 && w < extout - 1e-3 && dep < extin - 1e-3)) {
        i@_shapebad = 1;
        return;
    }
    vector p1 = box[at] - ein * dep;
    vector p2 = p1 + eout * w;
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        if (i != at) { push(ring, box[i]); continue; }
        push(ring, p1); push(ring, p2); push(ring, p2 + ein * dep);
    }
} else {
    // shapeU: remove a `widthM` x `depthM` rectangle centred on box side
    // `at`, cutting inward - two reflex corners instead of one.
    vector e = normalize(box[(at + 1) % 4] - box[at]);
    vector no = normalize(set(e.z, 0.0, -e.x));
    if (!(w > 1e-3 && dep > 1e-3 && w < extout - 1e-3
          && dep < extin - 1e-3)) {
        i@_shapebad = 1;
        return;
    }
    vector mid = (box[at] + box[(at + 1) % 4]) * 0.5;
    vector m1 = mid - e * (w * 0.5);
    vector m2 = m1 - no * dep;
    vector m3 = m2 + e * w;
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        push(ring, box[i]);
        if (i == at) { push(ring, m1); push(ring, m2); push(ring, m3);
                       push(ring, m3 + no * dep); }
    }
}

// ⛔ THE CONTAINMENT GUARD, OVER THE WHOLE OUTLINE AND NOT JUST ITS CORNERS.
// For one build it tested CORNERS ONLY and said so - a stated limitation whose
// MAGNITUDE nobody had measured, and the standing check shared the hole.
// Measured on an ORDINARY slotted parcel (a 30 x 24 lot with a 12 m slot bitten
// out of its +z edge) under `shapeL(6, 3, at = 2)`: all six footprint corners
// test inside-or-on, the guard passed, and the shipped footprint's +z edge ran
// 5.800 m OUTSIDE the lot for 12 m of its length with all four `pf_warn_*`
// at 0 - and `C.masses_inside_lots`, the standing check written for
// `build_retrospective.md` §2a instance 21, reported PASS on it too.
//
// ⛔ AND A PROPER-CROSSING TEST IS NOT THE FIX, MEASURED BEFORE IT SHIPPED.
// The first draft here asked whether a footprint edge properly CROSSES a lot
// edge, both segments straddling the other's line.  It reports the slotted lot
// above as contained: the footprint edge runs along z = 24 and the slot's two
// sides meet it exactly AT their endpoints (8, 24) and (2, 24), so it crosses
// nothing - it passes through two lot VERTICES.  That is not a degenerate
// corner case, it is what a slot bitten out of an edge the footprint runs along
// looks like every time.
//
// SO THE EDGE IS SPLIT AT EVERY PARAMETER WHERE IT MEETS THE LOT AT ALL, and
// each piece is tested at its MIDPOINT.  Between two consecutive meetings an
// edge is wholly inside or wholly outside, so the midpoints decide it exactly -
// no sample step, so no bulge and no slot is too narrow to see.  This subsumes
// the corner test: a corner outside the lot makes the piece next to it outside
// too, which is why there is one loop here and not two.
// INSIDE OR ON, for the reason the corner test had it: on a rectangle every box
// corner and every box edge lies exactly ON the lot line, which is legal and is
// what `setback(0)` asks for.  1e-3 m is two orders above float32 `P` at this
// domain and three below anything architectural.
// ⚠️ WHAT IS STILL NOT SEEN: a lot that is not a SIMPLE polygon - one that
// crosses itself, or arrives as two loops in one prim.  The in/out test is a
// crossing count, which answers a different question there.
int escaped = 0;
for (int k = 0; k < len(ring) && !escaped; k++) {
    vector a = ring[k], b = ring[(k + 1) % len(ring)];
    vector ab = set(b.x - a.x, 0.0, b.z - a.z);
    if (length(ab) < 1e-9) continue;
    float cut[] = array(0.0, 1.0);
    for (int e = 0; e < n; e++) {
        vector p = lot[e], r = lot[(e + 1) % n];
        vector pr = set(r.x - p.x, 0.0, r.z - p.z);
        float den = ab.x * pr.z - ab.z * pr.x;
        if (abs(den) < 1e-12) continue;
        float t = ((p.x - a.x) * pr.z - (p.z - a.z) * pr.x) / den;
        float u = ((p.x - a.x) * ab.z - (p.z - a.z) * ab.x) / den;
        if (t > 0.0 && t < 1.0 && u > -1e-9 && u < 1.0 + 1e-9) push(cut, t);
    }
    cut = sort(cut);
    for (int c = 0; c + 1 < len(cut) && !escaped; c++) {
        vector q = a + ab * ((cut[c] + cut[c + 1]) * 0.5);
        int inside = 0;
        float best = 1e18;
        for (int e = 0; e < n; e++) {
            vector p = lot[e], r = lot[(e + 1) % n];
            if ((p.z > q.z) != (r.z > q.z)
                && q.x < p.x + (q.z - p.z) * (r.x - p.x) / (r.z - p.z))
                inside = !inside;
            vector d = r - p;
            float l2 = d.x * d.x + d.z * d.z;
            float tt = (l2 < 1e-18) ? 0.0
                : clamp(((q.x - p.x) * d.x + (q.z - p.z) * d.z) / l2, 0.0, 1.0);
            best = min(best, length(set(q.x - p.x - d.x * tt, 0.0,
                                        q.z - p.z - d.z * tt)));
        }
        if (!inside && best > 1e-3) escaped = 1;
    }
}
if (escaped) { i@_shapebad = 1; return; }

// The box above is positively wound; a lot that arrived wound the other way
// keeps its winding, because `pf_area0` recomputes the sign downstream and a
// footprint that silently reversed would hand `pf_inset.vfl` the wrong side.
// ⛔ `reverse()` IS A PURE FUNCTION AND THE STATEMENT FORM IS A NO-OP.
// Measured on 22.0.398: `int a[] = array(1,2,3,4); reverse(a);` leaves
// `a[0] == 1`, while `b = reverse(b)` gives `b[0] == 4`.  For one build this
// line was `reverse(ring);` and did nothing, so a CLOCKWISE lot kept the
// positively-wound box while the role loop below multiplied its normals by the
// input's `sgn` of -1 - matching every output edge to the input edge facing the
// OPPOSITE way.  Every role, and therefore every setback, landed on the wrong
// edge and the result was still a perfectly legal L.
ring = (sgn < 0.0) ? reverse(ring) : ring;

int m = len(ring);
int npt[];
resize(npt, m);
for (int k = 0; k < m; k++) {
    int pt = (k < n) ? pts[k] : addpoint(0, ring[k]);
    setpointattrib(0, "P", pt, ring[k]);
    npt[k] = pt;
}
// The PRIMITIVE is kept and rewired rather than rebuilt, so every prim
// attribute `stamp()` wrote survives untouched.  Rebuilding would mean copying
// a dozen names by hand, and a copy list is a coupling that rots silently the
// first time a field is added to `stamp()`.
for (int k = n; k < m; k++) addvertex(0, @primnum, npt[k]);
for (int k = n - 1; k >= m; k--) {
    removevertex(0, vertexindex(0, @primnum, k));
    removepoint(0, pts[k], 0);
}
for (int k = 0; k < m; k++) setprimvertex(0, @primnum, k, npt[k]);

for (int k = 0; k < m; k++) {
    vector v = set(ring[(k + 1) % m].x - ring[k].x, 0.0,
                   ring[(k + 1) % m].z - ring[k].z);
    vector no = normalize(set(v.z, 0.0, -v.x)) * sgn;
    int ax = 0;
    float best = -2.0;
    for (int q = 0; q < 4; q++) {
        float dt = dot(no, axis[q]);
        if (dt > best) { best = dt; ax = q; }
    }
    int e = src[ax];
    setvertexattrib(0, "pf_face_role", @primnum, k, inrole[e]);
    setvertexattrib(0, "_inset", @primnum, k, ininset[e]);
    setvertexattrib(0, "pf_setback", @primnum, k, inback[e]);
}
